1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,368
Hello, Helen.

2
00:00:02,368 --> 00:00:03,340
Sorry I'm late.

3
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:04,709
Hi, Jeremy, no problem.

4
00:00:04,709 --> 00:00:09,160
Well we'd better work out where we are on our project, I suppose.

5
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:09,455
Yeah.

6
00:00:09,455 --> 00:00:12,999
I've looked at the drawings you've done for my story, 'The Forest',

7
00:00:12,999 --> 00:00:16,838
and I think they're brilliant - they really create the atmosphere I had in

8
00:00:16,838 --> 00:00:18,610
mind when I was writing it.

9
00:00:18,610 --> 00:00:20,230
I'm glad you like them.

10
00:00:20,230 --> 00:00:23,510
There are just a few suggestions I'd like to make.

11
00:00:23,510 --> 00:00:24,200
Go ahead.

12
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,154
Now, I'm not sure about the drawing of the cave - it's got trees

13
00:00:29,154 --> 00:00:30,297
all around it,

14
00:00:30,297 --> 00:00:31,440
which is great,

15
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:34,870
but the drawing a bit too static, isn' it?

16
00:00:34,870 --> 00:00:37,120
I think it needs some action.

17
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:39,050
Yes, there's nothing happening.

18
00:00:39,050 --> 00:00:42,706
Perhaps I should add the boy - Malcolm, isn't it?

19
00:00:42,706 --> 00:00:45,550
He would be walking up to it.

20
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:48,160
Yes, let's have Malcolm in the drawing.

21
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:53,008
And what about putting in a tiger - the one that he makes friends

22
00:00:53,008 --> 00:00:54,500
with a bit later?

23
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:58,680
Maybe it could be sitting under a tree washing itself.

24
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:02,724
And the tiger stops in the middle of what it's doing when it sees

25
00:01:02,724 --> 00:01:03,590
Malcolm walking past.

26
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:04,670
That's a good idea.

27
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:06,280
OK, I'll have a go at that.

28
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:09,960
Then there's the drawing of the crowd of men and women dancing.

29
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:11,815
They've just outside the forest,

30
00:01:11,815 --> 00:01:14,040
and there's a lot going on.

31
00:01:14,040 --> 00:01:17,513
That's right, you wanted them to be watching a carnival procession,

32
00:01:17,513 --> 00:01:20,040
but I thought it would be too crowded.

33
00:01:20,040 --> 00:01:22,250
Do you think it works like this?

34
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:24,787
Yes, I like what you've done.

35
00:01:24,787 --> 00:01:30,707
The only thing is, could you add Malcolm to it, without changing what's already

36
00:01:30,707 --> 00:01:31,130
there.

37
00:01:31,130 --> 00:01:35,965
What about having him sitting on the tree trunk on the right of the

38
00:01:35,965 --> 00:01:36,310
picture?

39
00:01:36,310 --> 00:01:36,798
Yes,

40
00:01:36,798 --> 00:01:38,750
that would be fine.

41
00:01:38,750 --> 00:01:41,430
And do you want him watching the other people?

42
00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:44,290
No, he's been left out of all the fun,

43
00:01:44,290 --> 00:01:48,422
so I'd like him to be crying - that'll contrast nicely with the next

44
00:01:48,422 --> 00:01:51,600
picture, where he's laughing at the clowns in the carnival.

45
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:53,060
Right, I'll do that.

46
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:56,970
And then the drawing of the people ice skating in the forest,

47
00:01:56,970 --> 00:01:59,070
I wasn't too happy with that one.

48
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:02,840
Because they've supposed to be skating on grass, aren't they?

49
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:03,624
That's right,

50
00:02:03,624 --> 00:02:05,193
and it's frozen over.

51
00:02:05,193 --> 00:02:08,330
At the moment it doesn't look quite right.

52
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:10,345
Mm, I see what you mean.

53
00:02:10,345 --> 00:02:12,360
I'll have another go at that.

54
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:15,450
And I like the wool hats they're wearing.

55
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:18,180
Maybe you could give each of them a scarf, as well.

56
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:18,558
Yeah,

57
00:02:18,558 --> 00:02:19,690
that's easy enough.

58
00:02:19,690 --> 00:02:24,010
They can be streaming out behind the people to suggest they're skating really fast.

59
00:02:24,010 --> 00:02:24,970
Mm, great.

60
00:02:24,970 --> 00:02:27,850
Well that's all on the drawings.

61
00:02:27,850 --> 00:02:28,259
Right.

62
00:02:28,259 --> 00:02:33,987
So you've finished writing your story and I just need to finish illustrating it,

63
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:37,260
and my story and your drawings are done.

64
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:43,229
So the next thing is to decide what exactly we need to write about

65
00:02:43,229 --> 00:02:46,639
in the report that goes with the stories,

66
00:02:46,639 --> 00:02:50,050
and how we're going to divide the work.

67
00:02:50,050 --> 00:02:50,690
Right, Helen.

68
00:02:50,690 --> 00:02:55,123
What do you think about including a section on how we planned the project

69
00:02:55,123 --> 00:02:56,390
as a whole, Jeremy?

70
00:02:56,390 --> 00:02:58,450
That's probably quite important.

71
00:02:58,450 --> 00:02:58,790
Yeah.

72
00:02:58,790 --> 00:03:02,190
Well, you've had most of the good ideas so far.

73
00:03:02,190 --> 00:03:06,950
How do you feel about drafting something, then we can go through it together

74
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:07,970
and discuss it?

75
00:03:07,970 --> 00:03:08,273
OK,

76
00:03:08,273 --> 00:03:09,182
that seems reasonable.

77
00:03:09,182 --> 00:03:13,425
And I could include something on how we came up with the ideas for

78
00:03:13,425 --> 00:03:14,940
our two stories, couldn't I?

79
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:17,393
Well I've started writing something about that,

80
00:03:17,393 --> 00:03:22,300
so why don't you do the same and we can include the two things.

81
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:22,720
Right.

82
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:26,080
So what about our interpretation of the stories?

83
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:29,836
Do we need to write about what we think they show, like the value

84
00:03:29,836 --> 00:03:32,250
of helping other people, all that sort of thing?

85
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:34,910
That's going to come up later, isn't it?

86
00:03:34,910 --> 00:03:39,775
I think everyone in the class is going to read each other's stories

87
00:03:39,775 --> 00:03:42,395
and come up with their own interpretations,

88
00:03:42,395 --> 00:03:44,640
which we are going to discuss.

89
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:45,818
Oh, I missed that.

90
00:03:45,818 --> 00:03:49,350
So it isn't going to be part of the report at all?

91
00:03:49,350 --> 00:03:49,738
No.

92
00:03:49,738 --> 00:03:52,843
But we need to write about the illustrations,

93
00:03:52,843 --> 00:03:57,500
because they're an essential element of children's experience of reading the stories.

94
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:02,501
It's probably easiest for you to write that section, as you know more about

95
00:04:02,501 --> 00:04:03,930
drawing than I do.

96
00:04:03,930 --> 00:04:04,248
Maybe,

97
00:04:04,248 --> 00:04:08,704
but I find it quite hard to write about, I'd be happier if you

98
00:04:08,704 --> 00:04:09,340
did it.

99
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:09,950
OK.

100
00:04:09,950 --> 00:04:13,000
So when do you think ...
